Following is a recursive Fibonacci implementation:
public int fib(int n) {
    if(n <= 1) return n;
    return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2);
}
When called with `fib(6)`, approximately how many function calls are made?

Scenario: The code finds all combinations of numbers that sum to target. What happens if we remove the backtracking line `addComb.remove(addComb.size()-1)`?

```
public void takeCombination(int index, int[] arr, int target, 
        List<List<Integer>> comb, List<Integer> addComb) {
    if(index == arr.length) {
        if(target == 0)
            comb.add(new ArrayList<>(addComb)); // Important: new ArrayList!
        return;
    }

    if(arr[index] <= target) {
        addComb.add(arr[index]);
        takeCombination(index, arr, target - arr[index], comb, addComb);
        addComb.remove(addComb.size()-1); // Remove this line - what happens?
    }

    takeCombination(index + 1, arr, target, comb, addComb);
}
```

Scenario: Given array `[5, 5, 5, 5]`, what will the following code output?

``
int max1 = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
int max2 = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

for(int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if(arr[i] > max1) {
        max2 = max1;
        max1 = arr[i];
    } else if(arr[i] > max2) {
        max2 = arr[i];
    }
}
System.out.println("Max: " + max1 + ", SecondMax: " + max2);
```

Scenario: The following code builds an undirected graph:

public void addEdge(int node1, int node2) {
    tree.putIfAbsent(node1, new ArrayList<>());
    tree.putIfAbsent(node2, new ArrayList<>());
    tree.get(node1).add(node2);
    tree.get(node2).add(node1);
}
```

When called with `addEdge(1, 1)` (adding edge from node to itself), what will be the structure?
